 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the internal storage layout. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, local index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite database stored on the kiosk. No certificate behavior or interface change depends on this storage choice.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+1. Verify the snapshot signature using the bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers (after normalization) for uniqueness.
+2. Build a new SQLite database in a staging path from the validated newline-delimited JSON records, indexing the normalized serial number for direct point lookups and storing the snapshot date in metadata.
+3. Finalize the staged database and atomically activate it (replacing or switching the active database pointer) only after all validation and index construction succeed.
+4. On any validation or build failure, discard the staging artifacts, leave the previous active SQLite snapshot untouched, and report a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the active read-only SQLite database by normalized serial number and return `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`, including the active snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory, managed search service | Satisfies the kiosk offline/no-network tenets, delivers predictable local lookups within the 250 ms budget for 90,000 records on 4 GB RAM, and keeps storage and index lifecycle private to the registry node with no external dependencies or credentials. |
